 2. 👀 GOP&#x27;s closing argument gets cheeky 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 President Trump seems to think he can save a vulnerable Ohio Republican senator's ass by talking about how strong it is, Axios' Stef W. Kight writes. 

 Why it matters: Republicans are getting hammered over the war in Iran, gas prices and data centers. But they're playing along with Trump's ogling of male candidates, which he's described as an election strategy. 💡 Between the lines: Trump is a showman. When he gets a reaction to something, he does more of it.

 Trump said of Sen. Jon Husted (R-Ohio), who was a college football star for the Dayton Flyers, at an Ohio rally: "I don't do this with men very often. I started examining his body because I didn't know he was a great athlete. And I looked, and I watched up and down, and I looked at his ass," since that's where his athletic power comes from. 

 Husted told Axios: "My wife and I ... have made sure to hit the gym during the busy campaign to uphold the high standards the president has set for us." President Trump campaigns with Sen. Jon Husted (R-Ohio) in Chillicothe, Ohio, yesterday. Photo: Kevin Dietsch/Getty Images 🔭 Zoom in: Over three weeks and five rallies across the country, Trump gave Husted a head-to-toe appraisal.

 "His ass was like a big piece of muscle," Trump said in Michigan last week. "Then I looked at his legs and I said, 'Man, his feet are big,'" Trump said in North Carolina. Trump has made similar comments about Rep. Mike Collins (R-Ga.), who's running for Senate, Georgia gubernatorial candidate Rick Jackson, Sen. Tim Sheehy (R-Mont.) and Kentucky House candidate Ed Gallrein. 

 Senate Majority Leader John Thune (R-S.D.) joined the fun: "I know that Husted gets a ton of attention, but I think [Sen.] Bernie [Moreno of Ohio] is kinda eye candy, too." Share this story . 
 
