 3. 🏔️ Alaska&#x27;s ballot blowup 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Alaska's ballot. Photo: Haven Daley/AP Campaign staffers for Alaska Democratic Senate candidate Mary Peltola helped recruit a challenger with the same name as incumbent Republican Sen. Dan Sullivan to draw votes away from him, The New York Times reported  yesterday, citing dozens of text messages. 

 The other Dan Sullivan, a retired teacher who will also appear on the ballot, registered as a Republican shortly before the filing deadline. The plan, which was internally called the ballot composition project, "involved the Peltola campaign's lawyers, as well as senior aides at the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee," the Times reports.

 Both the Peltola campaign and the DSCC denied involvement. Alaska Gov. Mike Dunleavy (R) ordered a criminal investigation of the campaign last night. 🔬 Zoom in: Former Peltola campaign manager Elisa Rios told the Times that Jerid Kurtz, a Democratic operative from Ohio, pitched would-be candidates over beers.

 Rios maintained that Peltola herself wasn't directly involved in the project but was "definitely aware." Kurtz — who went by the alias "Zane" while working in Alaska — also urged a Democrat who'd made the ballot to drop out, clearing a spot for another Republican. The intrigue: Rios, who was fired in August and had planned to sue the campaign for wrongful termination, tried last week to retract her interviews. 

 The paper went ahead, saying the article was also based on interviews with 11 other people: "The Times had never agreed that those previous interviews could not be published, and informed Ms. Rios last week that they would be a part of this story." President Trump told reporters : "Mary Peltola should resign from the race" and "should be prosecuted criminally for what she did."

