 &nbsp; 
 
 
 5. ⚠️ Exclusive: Ramaswamy ad backfires 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Republican Vivek Ramaswamy campaigns in Strongsville, Ohio, last week. Photo: Jeff Swensen/Getty Images Republicans fear Vivek Ramaswamy's explosive attack ad featuring his opponent's estranged mother could cost them the Ohio governor's race, based on new internal polling that shows him falling further behind, Axios' Alex Isenstadt writes. 

 National Republican operatives think the backlash against Ramaswamy could also hurt Ohio Sen. Jon Husted's reelection bid. 📊 A memo attached to the internal poll, commissioned by a Republican group and obtained by Axios, found that 86% of the survey's 800 respondents said they had seen, read or heard something about the ad.

 By a 2-to-1 ratio, those who said they were familiar with it said it made them more likely to vote for Democrat Amy Acton. The poll found Acton leading Ramaswamy by eight points — up from a two-point lead in the same poll earlier this month, before the ad began circulating on Oct. 19. The ad at the center of the controversy features Donna Arthur, Acton's estranged mother, who accuses her daughter of lying about her childhood.

 Acton has said she was sexually abused by her stepfather when she was 12 and that her mother sided with her abuser. The stepfather was charged with sexual assault but fled to Pennsylvania. Share this story . 

 
 
