 6. 📈 RAISE US wins big new funding 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 RAISE US, cofounded by Gina Raimondo, convened 50+ leaders from tech, organized labor, higher ed and government at Brown University on Monday. Photo : RAISE US RAISE US, a well-funded, well-connected project to help American workers transition to an AI economy, today announced huge new funding from Apple, AT&amp;T, the Bezos family, Google, and Walmart, bringing total commitments to the four-month-old, nonpartisan group to over $735 million.

 Why it matters: The backing shows a new way corporate America is trying to get ahead of looming AI-related economic and political disruption.

 Former Commerce Secretary Gina Raimondo, a RAISE US cofounder, told me the new support will "allow us to expand and accelerate our efforts coast to coast — working with states and employers to grow apprenticeship and early career training programs, retrain and redeploy mid-career workers, and strengthen the social safety net for Americans whose jobs are affected by AI." The group was launched in June by Raimondo, a Democrat and former Rhode Island governor, and former Indiana Gov. Eric Holcomb, with a goal of $1 billion in multiyear commitments.

 The group isn't disclosing specific donations, but the new total is a jump of more than $200 million since the group's initial announcement. The RAISE US Policy Lab will test ideas for supporting, retraining and redeploying workers, and turn those learnings into recommendations for decision-makers in Washington, state capitols, and the private sector. 
 
